1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
And now the old scout master.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:13,680
This is no joke, okay, This is true. Years ago, I taught Principal for Navigation for

3
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:17,240
the Department of National Resources for the Hunter Education class, because we were picking

4
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:23,180
up a lot of lost hunters every year, And so they developed this questionnaire where they

5
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:27,840
would ask these lost hunters a battery of different questions, And one of the questions

6
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:34,640
they asked them was: do you carry a compass? And most of them did. And so then

7
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:40,080
the next question was: well, you know, why didn't you use the compass to, you know,

8
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:44,879
find your way out of the woods? And the answer was: well, you know, I tried, but

9
00:00:44,879 --> 00:00:49,760
I don't know how. And then the next question was: well, if you don't know how,

10
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:53,760
then why do you carry a compass? And the answer- believe it or not- was:

11
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:59,839
everyone should carry a compass in case they get lost. I don't know if that's a

12
00:00:59,839 --> 00:01:06,480
joke or not, but it's a true story. And this is podcast number 182..

13
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:53,400
Welcome back to the Scout Master Podcast. This is Clark Green. If you're listening to the podcast in the week that is published, right around mid-August in 2013,, I am paddling my way through the wilds of Algonquin

14
00:01:53,400 --> 00:02:12,000
Provincial Park up in Ontario, Canada, with our Scouts And I wanted to get a podcast scheduled to go out on the Monday. I'm Away in my absence And we are going to listen to one of my favorite interviews. that goes way, way back to podcast number 54. And that's with a

15
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:40,200
distinguished Eagle Scout author and canoe guru, Cliff Jacobson. Cliff would be well known to a lot of us through his articles in Boys Life and some of the books that he wrote, And we'll be talking about all that when I interview Cliff And that's going to take up the remainder of this podcast. But before we go to the interview, have a few things from the mailbag to share over on iTunes. We're catching up on a couple of comments that were left.

16
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:56,040
Jim Crutch said Clark is serving us all well by advocating for the scouting program in a simplified and funny way that makes it easy to understand. If you're an adult involved with the troop level in Boy Scouts you should be listening to this podcast, Jim. thanks for your kind comments there.

17
00:02:56,359 --> 00:03:22,359
Also from Mike Stock, who is a scout master with Troop 620 on iTunes. He says I've been listening to Clark's podcast since my son crossed over four years ago. Since then my son became the SPL and I've become the scout master. Clark's information inside of made me a better leader and given me extremely positive effect on how our troop operates. I can't overstate just how great a resource this is. We're better

18
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:34,040
scouters and a better troop because of this podcast. If you want a great source of information on scouting is how scouting is supposed to be done, then look no further. Thanks, Mike. Wow, I've got a lot to live up to now.

19
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:51,920
Also heard from Ben Davidson, and Ben is with the Goodwick Sea Scout group in Wales in the United Kingdom. He wrote in to say this: I found your website a couple of months ago and what a great resource it is. Although your concentration is on American scouting, it's amazing how good

20
00:03:51,920 --> 00:04:21,720
things and hard things that occur with your scout organization are the same as we experience here in the UK. Keep up the good work and the enthusiasm for scouting that you very clearly have. From a scouting fan in the UK, kind regards. thanks so much. It's my honor to have a number of listeners in the UK and in Canada and in Australia as well as in the United States, And you are. you hit the nail on the head. We all basically experience the same things. We may have some differences in the way our

21
00:04:21,720 --> 00:04:49,360
organization's work and administrative things, But scouting, it's a worldwide movement And anywhere, in any language, in just about any culture, most scout leaders are going to be encountering the exact same things that other scout leaders all over the world are encountering, whether it be good or difficult or challenging or rewarding. You know, it's just wonderful And that's part of the genius of scouting and why it's so great to be involved.

22
00:04:49,360 --> 00:05:03,200
Thanks again, Ben. As I promised, we're going to replay the interview I did back in podcast number 54 with Cliff Jacobson. I think you'll really enjoy it. So let's get started, shall we?

23
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:40,360
Today I'm speaking with Wilderness guide Cliff Jacobson, who's the author of over a dozen top selling books on camping and canoeing. Cliff's an environmental science teacher who retired after 34 years of teaching, And he's the 2003 recipient of the Legends of Paddling Award from the American Canoe Association.

24
00:05:40,360 --> 00:06:00,360
As a distinguished Eagle Scout, Cliff continues to contribute to scouting through his articles in scouting magazine And, as a matter of fact, if you look in the most current edition, that's the January- February 2011 edition, you're going to find an article by Cliff about building fires in the rain, And Cliff joins me today from his home in River Falls, Wisconsin. Cliff, how are you doing?

25
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:19,360
Hey, yeah, listen, man, listen, I'm doing great here. Listen, I appreciate the nice intro, but I just got to say something about thank you for mentioning the latest issue of scouting magazine. But for your listeners, I think that particular article is a very interesting and unusual one,

26
00:06:20,360 --> 00:06:47,360
And why? Because if you look at the picture in there and you read it, it talks about an axe. You know how traditionally the Scouts have felt about axes, And it took a lot of convincing for me to get the boys down in Texas to agree that it was important to be able to build a fire, to be able to use an axe in that matter,

27
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:51,360
And so I think that's a real plus.

28
00:06:51,360 --> 00:06:53,360
I think so too,

29
00:06:53,360 --> 00:07:00,360
And I think Scouts, as you embrace that method, will find that it's perfectly safe and you'll always be able to get a fire going for your boys.

30
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:11,360
Well, you know, a lot of times people look at a teenage boy and an axe and they put those two things together and it spells catastrophe. but that ain't necessarily so. we both know that,

31
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:15,360
Yeah, but anyway, I just thought I would mention it. but, thank you,

32
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:22,360
I'm really curious about your time in scouting. Tell me a little bit about where you were scouting and where you grew up.

33
00:07:22,360 --> 00:07:26,360
Well, actually, I grew up and I was born in Chicago-

34
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:27,360
Oh no, kidding,

35
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:34,360
Yeah- and I lived in right in the heart of Chicago in a third-story apartment building and I hated every minute of it.

36
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:43,360
As long as I can remember, I had in my blood this idea that I wanted to go camping, that I wanted to be outside, and I wanted to be outdoors camping.

37
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:49,360
And Chicago didn't have it. And the thing that did was the Boy Scouts.

38
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:56,360
And so I embraced the Boy Scouts with full gusto and I belonged to one of those troops, troop 21..

39
00:07:56,360 --> 00:08:02,360
I still remember troop 21 and we went camping once a month, rain or shine,

40
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:05,360
And that was absolutely the highlight of my life,

41
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:11,360
Greatest compliment that anybody ever gave me with. a scout master once said to me:

42
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:16,360
Cliff, he says I've read your books. He said- and I don't know who you think you're kidding-

43
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:21,360
He says all you've done is taken all that old Boy Scout stuff and modernized it.

44
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:25,360
And I said she promised me you won't tell a soul.

45
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:28,360
Yeah, I owe a lot to scouting.

46
00:08:28,360 --> 00:08:33,360
What do you think kept you involved and interested in scouts as much as you were?

47
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:37,360
Oh, it was the camping period. It was totally the camping.

48
00:08:37,360 --> 00:08:42,360
And you know, we used to have a saying and I think most scouts still remember:

49
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:45,360
you take the outing out of scouting, you take the scouting out of the boy.

50
00:08:45,360 --> 00:08:46,360
Is that still a thing?

51
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:48,360
Yeah, it's still out there.

52
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:51,360
Yeah, that to me is the major thing.

53
00:08:51,360 --> 00:08:58,360
I mean because you don't face it, nobody is taking kids in the outdoors today.

54
00:08:58,360 --> 00:09:06,360
Just think about that. Scouting is literally the last place on earth that takes kids out camping.

55
00:09:06,360 --> 00:09:12,360
I mean, sure, there's some summer camps, but that's a once a year shot for a week or so

56
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:15,360
and then you're done with scouting. It's a lifestyle.

57
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:19,360
So you know, don't minimize it. I would say to scout leaders:

58
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:23,360
Boy, don't minimize the importance of taking kids on camping trips.

59
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:28,360
I would say that's the most important thing you can do is take them out on camping trips.

60
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:32,360
So when you were a scout, I mean you didn't go and scale mountains

61
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:35,360
or run rivers or anything like that, You just went out camping.

62
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:41,360
Yeah, we went out camping. We would just go out in the woods. that we used to have in those days.

63
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:45,360
Now this would be considered blasphemy today, but it was so fun when I was a kid.

64
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:50,360
Now, imagine a group of 14-year-olds, 13-14-year-olds, going on a rifle hike.

65
00:09:50,360 --> 00:09:56,360
We would take our 22 rifles and we'd go in the woods with our leaders and we'd just shoot at the top,

66
00:09:56,360 --> 00:10:02,360
We'd just plank, you know, and we would have a couple of rifle hikes a year.

67
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:06,360
And there's no way you could do anything like that today.

68
00:10:06,360 --> 00:10:07,360
I'm afraid not.

69
00:10:07,360 --> 00:10:09,360
But I have vivid memories of the rifle hike.

70
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:16,360
You weren't too worried about where you went, You weren't too worried about some kind of, you know,

71
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:19,360
excellent program or diversion or something like that.

72
00:10:19,360 --> 00:10:20,360
Right,

73
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:22,360
What you wanted to do was get out and sleep in a tent.

74
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:27,360
Yeah, that's exactly it. And I mean people talk about going light today.

75
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:30,360
Are you kidding me? I mean we went light

76
00:10:30,360 --> 00:10:34,360
When we went. we would hike, We'd do 20-mile hikes when I was like 13 years old

77
00:10:34,360 --> 00:10:39,360
And I think my pack- I think if my pack- weighed 15 pounds, that was probably a lot

78
00:10:39,360 --> 00:10:46,360
And it did everything I needed. You know it had my sleeping bag, my poncho, a little cook hit thing

79
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:49,360
and a knife, And I had a little hatchet done,

80
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:50,360
Right,

81
00:10:50,360 --> 00:10:53,360
And I think, you know, maybe I had a sweater or something like that.

82
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:55,360
That was pretty much it.

83
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:58,360
You know, you built a fire, You put up your poncho

84
00:10:58,360 --> 00:11:03,360
And if it rained. well, you just learned how to rig a tarp so that you didn't get wet.

85
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:05,360
And if you did get wet, well, it was part of the game.

86
00:11:05,360 --> 00:11:07,360
Then you know, we had a lot of fun, though,

87
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:11,360
And that's why boys joined scouting, because that's what they want to do

88
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:12,360
Exactly.

89
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:15,360
So you went on to become an Eagle Scout.

90
00:11:15,360 --> 00:11:16,360
Yeah,

91
00:11:16,360 --> 00:11:19,360
Did I have the date, the year, right: 1956?

92
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:22,360
Yeah, that's right, 1956, yeah,

93
00:11:22,360 --> 00:11:26,360
And what do you remember about that whole process? putting that all together,

94
00:11:26,360 --> 00:11:29,360
Man, you know, I was actually thinking about that.

95
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:31,360
I remember, of course it was 21,. Merrick Badges,

96
00:11:31,360 --> 00:11:32,360
Is it still 21?

97
00:11:32,360 --> 00:11:33,360
It's 21,. yeah,

98
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:34,360
Still 21..

99
00:11:34,360 --> 00:11:39,360
I vividly remember flunking life-saving three times.

100
00:11:39,360 --> 00:11:41,360
Three times.

101
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:42,360
That was a summer camp,

102
00:11:42,360 --> 00:11:46,360
And so it took me two years to get it. to get life-saving,

103
00:11:46,360 --> 00:11:48,360
You had to have a life-saving.

104
00:11:48,360 --> 00:11:51,360
There was no way getting out of swimming and life-saving-

105
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:55,360
If you couldn't swim, you couldn't get your Eagle. in those days,

106
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:57,360
You had to have optional badges,

107
00:11:57,360 --> 00:12:01,360
And I think it's good now that we do for people who can't.

108
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:03,360
I don't remember a service project-

109
00:12:03,360 --> 00:12:05,360
Now, maybe I did one,

110
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:06,360
I don't remember it.

111
00:12:06,360 --> 00:12:09,360
I don't remember in 1956 if we had one or not.

112
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:14,360
There's a lot of guys who would know who will. let me know who are listening to this.

113
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:15,360
Yeah, that would be interesting.

114
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:18,360
You know, maybe I had one, but it was so long ago

115
00:12:18,360 --> 00:12:23,360
And I also remember boys were generally older when they got their Eagles in.

116
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:26,360
There were very few Eagles before 15..

117
00:12:26,360 --> 00:12:28,360
I was 16..

118
00:12:28,360 --> 00:12:30,360
That was a pretty average age then.

119
00:12:30,360 --> 00:12:32,360
That's still my experience today.

120
00:12:32,360 --> 00:12:34,360
16 actually is a little young,

121
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:38,360
At least in my experience. they end up dragging their feet until they're 17 and a half.

122
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:47,360
Well, that's okay too, because I think you get a lot more experiences as you get older.

123
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:50,360
But yeah, it was definitely the camping that drew me to Scouting.

124
00:12:50,360 --> 00:12:53,360
In the summer I went to summer camp every summer

125
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:56,360
And that's how I got into the canoeing.

126
00:12:56,360 --> 00:12:58,360
It was in summer camp.

127
00:12:58,360 --> 00:13:03,360
Yeah, you talk about that a little bit in your introduction in one of your books, Expedition Canoing.

128
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:08,360
I guess your first experiences were in one of the great old wood canvas canoes.

129
00:13:08,360 --> 00:13:09,360
Oh, yeah, sure,

130
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:11,360
I put a lot of miles on those babies-

131
00:13:11,360 --> 00:13:15,360
Woodward Thompson's in Townsend, Old Towns, Shell Lakes.

132
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:18,360
So that's where I learned how to paddle.

133
00:13:18,360 --> 00:13:22,360
Everything I learned in the outdoors I basically learned in two ways:

134
00:13:22,360 --> 00:13:26,360
One from the Boy Scouts and two from reading all the books in print on the subject.

135
00:13:26,360 --> 00:13:28,360
I just went out and did it.

136
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:30,360
Do you remember what camp it was?

137
00:13:30,360 --> 00:13:32,360
Evanston Council's, Camp Wabaningo,

138
00:13:32,360 --> 00:13:33,360
I remember that.

139
00:13:33,360 --> 00:13:36,360
And the Chicago Council was a waspy

140
00:13:36,360 --> 00:13:40,360
And there was three camps. but I went to Camp West.

141
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:41,360
I still remember

142
00:13:41,360 --> 00:13:44,360
And the Chicago Council camp was more rugged.

143
00:13:44,360 --> 00:13:51,360
Then the Evanston Council camp was more frilly, nicer, mess hall,

144
00:13:51,360 --> 00:13:54,360
You stayed in a cabin, type things.

145
00:13:54,360 --> 00:13:58,360
But in the Chicago camp you were intense the whole time.

146
00:13:58,360 --> 00:14:00,360
I really liked the Chicago Council camp better.

147
00:14:00,360 --> 00:14:01,360
It was more rugged.

148
00:14:01,360 --> 00:14:09,360
Making things more modern and making things easier is not the answer.

149
00:14:09,360 --> 00:14:11,360
It's not, really not what kids want.

150
00:14:11,360 --> 00:14:14,360
They don't want to. adults may want to sleep in a cabin.

151
00:14:14,360 --> 00:14:16,360
Kids don't want to sleep in a cabin.

152
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:19,360
They may tell you, yeah, I want to sleep in a cabin,

153
00:14:19,360 --> 00:14:20,360
But they really don't.

154
00:14:20,360 --> 00:14:22,360
They want to be outside.

155
00:14:22,360 --> 00:14:30,360
We found the same thing when we took kids on canoe trips in the boundary waters in Ontario.

156
00:14:30,360 --> 00:14:33,360
We give them a real rough trip.

157
00:14:33,360 --> 00:14:38,360
They're complaining like man when they're out there canoeing in the rain

158
00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:41,360
and hooping all that gear over those portage trails.

159
00:14:41,360 --> 00:14:45,360
But, boy, with the end of the trip you just listen to them talk.

160
00:14:45,360 --> 00:14:47,360
Oh, this was so cool man.

161
00:14:47,360 --> 00:14:52,360
So at the outset, if you ask these kids, they're going to tell you they want the easy way,

162
00:14:52,360 --> 00:14:54,360
But deep down they don't.

163
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:55,360
They want the challenges,

164
00:14:55,360 --> 00:14:58,360
No matter when it was in the 50s or even right now. today,

165
00:14:58,360 --> 00:15:03,360
they rise to that challenge when they're given the latitude to be able to take it on.

166
00:15:03,360 --> 00:15:06,360
And I tell you, it's not just boys either,

167
00:15:06,360 --> 00:15:07,360
It's girls too.

168
00:15:07,360 --> 00:15:12,360
So I think one of the smartest things that the Scouts did was include the Venture Program

169
00:15:12,360 --> 00:15:16,360
and get girls involved, because they want to do the same thing.

170
00:15:16,360 --> 00:15:19,360
You did go on. after you became an Eagle Scout,

171
00:15:19,360 --> 00:15:22,360
You went on to become a teacher.

172
00:15:22,360 --> 00:15:24,360
Well, actually I was a forester first.

173
00:15:24,360 --> 00:15:27,360
I graduated in forestry and then I worked as a forester

174
00:15:27,360 --> 00:15:30,360
for the Bureau of Land Management in Oregon.

175
00:15:30,360 --> 00:15:32,360
And then after that I went in the Army

176
00:15:32,360 --> 00:15:37,360
And when I was in the Army I did actually quite a bit of teaching military subjects

177
00:15:37,360 --> 00:15:39,360
And I said, gee, this teaching is kind of fun.

178
00:15:39,360 --> 00:15:44,360
So then I went back to school, got a certificate to teach biology

179
00:15:44,360 --> 00:15:47,360
and taught that for a number of years and then came to Minnesota

180
00:15:47,360 --> 00:15:50,360
and taught environmental science for the rest of the time.

181
00:15:50,360 --> 00:15:54,360
You were teaching in Minnesota for all those 34 years.

182
00:15:54,360 --> 00:15:57,360
No, I taught in Indiana. for what was that?

183
00:15:57,360 --> 00:15:59,360
I think it was five years in Indiana.

184
00:15:59,360 --> 00:16:04,360
I came to Minnesota and I taught 29 years in Minnesota.

185
00:16:04,360 --> 00:16:08,360
The trend was moving closer and closer to good canoe places. it sounds like

186
00:16:08,360 --> 00:16:10,360
You know you hit it on the head.

187
00:16:10,360 --> 00:16:15,360
That is exactly what happened, because at the end of my 50-year teaching in Indiana,

188
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:17,360
I wanted to come to Minnesota.

189
00:16:17,360 --> 00:16:20,360
I wanted to see this place called the Boundary Water Center.

190
00:16:20,360 --> 00:16:21,360
It was so magical.

191
00:16:21,360 --> 00:16:24,360
I took a canoe trip up here and I just fell in love with the area.

192
00:16:24,360 --> 00:16:26,360
I said I've got to get closer north,

193
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:27,360
I've got to get further north.

194
00:16:27,360 --> 00:16:28,360
So I get there.

195
00:16:28,360 --> 00:16:29,360
I'm moving to Minnesota.

196
00:16:29,360 --> 00:16:34,360
I sent out 350 job applications

197
00:16:34,360 --> 00:16:36,360
And then I got two job offers,

198
00:16:36,360 --> 00:16:39,360
One in Hastings, Minnesota, and one in Portage, Wisconsin.

199
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:42,360
And so the rest is history.

200
00:16:42,360 --> 00:16:43,360
You became an author.

201
00:16:43,360 --> 00:16:45,360
How did that come about?

202
00:16:45,360 --> 00:16:48,360
Well, becoming a writer was really accidental.

203
00:16:48,360 --> 00:16:51,360
I picked up a magazine and had an article in it called

204
00:16:51,360 --> 00:16:53,360
Canoing with Rob Roy.

205
00:16:53,360 --> 00:16:54,360
It was terrible.

206
00:16:54,360 --> 00:16:57,360
I said this guy doesn't know what he's talking about.

207
00:16:57,360 --> 00:17:01,360
So my wife said, well, if you can do better, go ahead.

208
00:17:01,360 --> 00:17:06,360
So I sat down and I wrote the definitive guide to Canoing.

209
00:17:06,360 --> 00:17:10,360
It was about 5,000 words

210
00:17:10,360 --> 00:17:14,360
And I sent it to Harry Roberts, who had a magazine then called

211
00:17:14,360 --> 00:17:16,360
Will and the Scamping,

212
00:17:16,360 --> 00:17:18,360
And Harry published it.

213
00:17:18,360 --> 00:17:21,360
The next thing that happened was I was teaching school

214
00:17:21,360 --> 00:17:24,360
and the Department of Natural Resources had a bunch of

215
00:17:24,360 --> 00:17:27,360
activities for teachers, one of which was called Fire Building.

216
00:17:27,360 --> 00:17:30,360
Well, in this particular activity there's

217
00:17:30,360 --> 00:17:33,360
you go out and just collect a variety of woods and things

218
00:17:33,360 --> 00:17:34,360
and see what burns best.

219
00:17:34,360 --> 00:17:36,360
And I complained about it.

220
00:17:36,360 --> 00:17:38,360
I said this is terrible advice.

221
00:17:38,360 --> 00:17:41,360
DNR. people said, well, why don't you rewrite it for us?

222
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:42,360
I said okay, I will

223
00:17:42,360 --> 00:17:43,360
And I rewrote it.

224
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:45,360
And then they called me and they said: see,

225
00:17:45,360 --> 00:17:46,360
this is really good.

226
00:17:46,360 --> 00:17:47,360
We didn't have to do anything to it.

227
00:17:47,360 --> 00:17:48,360
You want to do more work for us?

228
00:17:48,360 --> 00:17:49,360
I said yeah,

229
00:17:49,360 --> 00:17:51,360
And so that's kind of like how it started

230
00:17:51,360 --> 00:17:55,360
And it just sort of mushroomed into that.

231
00:17:55,360 --> 00:17:57,360
How long have you been writing for Scouting Magazine,

232
00:17:57,360 --> 00:17:58,360
Do you figure?

233
00:17:58,360 --> 00:18:01,360
Well, I've been wrote for Boys' Life for a while.

234
00:18:01,360 --> 00:18:03,360
Scouting is relatively new,

235
00:18:03,360 --> 00:18:06,360
I think just the last couple of years.

236
00:18:06,360 --> 00:18:09,360
But you know, I'm really thrilled to write

237
00:18:09,360 --> 00:18:12,360
for Scouting Magazine and Boys' Life.

238
00:18:12,360 --> 00:18:14,360
First of all, it's an honor,

239
00:18:14,360 --> 00:18:15,360
It really is.

240
00:18:15,360 --> 00:18:18,360
Of all the audiences that I work with,

241
00:18:18,360 --> 00:18:21,360
Scouters are the most fun.

242
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:23,360
Scouters are looking for knowledge.

243
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:28,360
Scouters are one of the few groups on the planet

244
00:18:28,360 --> 00:18:31,360
that understand that skills are more important than things.

245
00:18:31,360 --> 00:18:33,360
You don't need a lot of expensive stuff

246
00:18:33,360 --> 00:18:35,360
if you know what you're doing,

247
00:18:35,360 --> 00:18:38,360
They're not looking to go out and buy something new

248
00:18:38,360 --> 00:18:41,360
to solve their problems.

249
00:18:41,360 --> 00:18:44,360
Yeah, I really enjoy writing for the Scouting Magazine

250
00:18:44,360 --> 00:18:49,360
and sharing stuff like that for that reason.

251
00:18:49,360 --> 00:18:52,360
And you also contributed a section to

252
00:18:52,360 --> 00:18:54,360
one of the field books too- right,

253
00:18:54,360 --> 00:18:56,360
Yeah, the canoeing part.

254
00:18:56,360 --> 00:19:01,360
I've got two of your books that I keep within arms reach

255
00:19:01,360 --> 00:19:02,360
and handy.

256
00:19:02,360 --> 00:19:04,360
One of them is Expedition Canoing.

257
00:19:04,360 --> 00:19:05,360
Well, that was my Bible.

258
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:07,360
That's my all-time favorite book.

259
00:19:07,360 --> 00:19:09,360
You first published it in 1984

260
00:19:09,360 --> 00:19:13,360
and now it's in its fourth, 20th anniversary edition.

261
00:19:13,360 --> 00:19:16,360
While you do talk about gear and the choice of gear

262
00:19:16,360 --> 00:19:17,360
and things like that,

263
00:19:17,360 --> 00:19:21,360
you're not overly fascinated with it

264
00:19:21,360 --> 00:19:25,360
and you really encourage people to build a set of skills.

265
00:19:25,360 --> 00:19:28,360
There are two things that keep you alive in the outdoors:

266
00:19:28,360 --> 00:19:31,360
Skills and judgment.

267
00:19:31,360 --> 00:19:34,360
If you have few skills and a lot of judgment,

268
00:19:34,360 --> 00:19:36,360
you can survive,

269
00:19:36,360 --> 00:19:38,360
And if you have lots and lots of skills

270
00:19:38,360 --> 00:19:40,360
and not so good at judging,

271
00:19:40,360 --> 00:19:42,360
the skills might get you by.

272
00:19:42,360 --> 00:19:44,360
There's a book called Deep Survival

273
00:19:44,360 --> 00:19:47,360
which I would recommend your listeners to read.

274
00:19:47,360 --> 00:19:49,360
It's basically. the premise of the book

275
00:19:49,360 --> 00:19:52,360
is: how is it that under severe conditions,

276
00:19:52,360 --> 00:19:55,360
some people survive and others perish?

277
00:19:55,360 --> 00:19:58,360
And one example in there that he tells,

278
00:19:58,360 --> 00:20:00,360
which I think sort of tells it all:

279
00:20:00,360 --> 00:20:01,360
20 years ago or sometime ago,

280
00:20:01,360 --> 00:20:04,360
there was a huge storm in one of the western rivers.

281
00:20:04,360 --> 00:20:08,360
If you rafted the river at reasonable water levels,

282
00:20:08,360 --> 00:20:09,360
it was fun.

283
00:20:09,360 --> 00:20:12,360
If you got too high and terribly dangerous.

284
00:20:12,360 --> 00:20:15,360
and there were a number of groups on the river that day.

285
00:20:15,360 --> 00:20:18,360
they were all professionally guided groups of rafters

286
00:20:18,360 --> 00:20:21,360
and there was this one guy from someplace out east-

287
00:20:21,360 --> 00:20:23,360
I can't remember, he was a pastor,

288
00:20:23,360 --> 00:20:25,360
I can't remember what else about him-

289
00:20:25,360 --> 00:20:27,360
And they were halfway down the river

290
00:20:27,360 --> 00:20:29,360
and they were camped out and that night it rained

291
00:20:29,360 --> 00:20:31,360
and rained, and rained, and rained, and rained.

292
00:20:31,360 --> 00:20:33,360
and they woke up the following morning

293
00:20:33,360 --> 00:20:35,360
and the professional rafters looked at the river.

294
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:37,360
the professional guys looked at the river

295
00:20:37,360 --> 00:20:38,360
15 feet and said,

296
00:20:38,360 --> 00:20:41,360
well, we've never run at this high,

297
00:20:41,360 --> 00:20:43,360
but it should be fun,

298
00:20:43,360 --> 00:20:44,360
Okay.

299
00:20:44,360 --> 00:20:47,360
The pastor looked at the river and he said,

300
00:20:47,360 --> 00:20:51,360
whoa, this does not look safe to me.

301
00:20:51,360 --> 00:20:53,360
We're not going anywhere, boys,

302
00:20:53,360 --> 00:20:54,360
We're staying put.

303
00:20:54,360 --> 00:20:58,360
He stayed put and all of his people in his charge were fine

304
00:20:58,360 --> 00:21:01,360
and virtually every one of those raft trips

305
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:04,360
had multiple cap sizes and drownings.

306
00:21:04,360 --> 00:21:06,360
And so the question is:

307
00:21:06,360 --> 00:21:08,360
how is it that these experienced rafters

308
00:21:08,360 --> 00:21:10,360
that had been down this river hundreds

309
00:21:10,360 --> 00:21:14,360
or thousands of times chose to run at that day,

310
00:21:14,360 --> 00:21:18,360
and this pastor, who was a pretty decent rafter

311
00:21:18,360 --> 00:21:20,360
but had nowhere near that experience,

312
00:21:20,360 --> 00:21:23,360
chose to stay put,

313
00:21:23,360 --> 00:21:27,360
And that's one of the key things to survival.

314
00:21:27,360 --> 00:21:31,360
When I was a forester out in Oregon,

315
00:21:31,360 --> 00:21:35,360
I was a dumb kid, just 21 years old,

316
00:21:35,360 --> 00:21:37,360
and this is Western Oregon,

317
00:21:37,360 --> 00:21:41,360
And if you step off the path in Western Oregon

318
00:21:41,360 --> 00:21:44,360
you're in road of dendron and Douglas fir trees

319
00:21:44,360 --> 00:21:46,360
and I mean they turn you around.

320
00:21:46,360 --> 00:21:47,360
you'll never find your way out.

321
00:21:47,360 --> 00:21:49,360
I was putting in a timber sale

322
00:21:49,360 --> 00:21:50,360
or changing a timber sale,

323
00:21:50,360 --> 00:21:53,360
and you were supposed to change it by ribbiting in.

324
00:21:53,360 --> 00:21:56,360
You would walk in and you had this yellow flagging

325
00:21:56,360 --> 00:21:58,360
that you would put on the trees

326
00:21:58,360 --> 00:22:01,360
that would show where the boundaries of the timber sale is.

327
00:22:01,360 --> 00:22:04,360
But anyway, most normal people would start at the beginning

328
00:22:04,360 --> 00:22:06,360
and that they would walk to the end

329
00:22:06,360 --> 00:22:07,360
and then they would turn around

330
00:22:07,360 --> 00:22:09,360
and they would take off ribbons as they came back.

331
00:22:09,360 --> 00:22:10,360
Right,

332
00:22:10,360 --> 00:22:11,360
I didn't do that.

333
00:22:11,360 --> 00:22:14,360
I started at the beginning, started pulling off ribbons

334
00:22:14,360 --> 00:22:18,360
And pretty soon I was in there for about-

335
00:22:18,360 --> 00:22:20,360
oh, I guess about an hour

336
00:22:20,360 --> 00:22:23,360
and all of a sudden I realized I didn't know where I was,

337
00:22:23,360 --> 00:22:26,360
I didn't know what was going on, totally lost.

338
00:22:26,360 --> 00:22:28,360
And I had my silver compass

339
00:22:28,360 --> 00:22:32,360
and I had some water and a sandwich

340
00:22:32,360 --> 00:22:35,360
and I said I know the only way out of here is go due west,

341
00:22:35,360 --> 00:22:38,360
and I will eventually hit highway 101 in the ocean.

342
00:22:38,360 --> 00:22:40,360
And that's what I did.

343
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:41,360
I walked for three days

344
00:22:41,360 --> 00:22:43,360
and on the third day

345
00:22:43,360 --> 00:22:45,360
morning or the third day,

346
00:22:45,360 --> 00:22:47,360
I came out to a little town called Remote Oregon,

347
00:22:47,360 --> 00:22:49,360
with the name of the town,

348
00:22:49,360 --> 00:22:53,360
and I was picked up by a trucker on a log truck

349
00:22:53,360 --> 00:22:56,360
who took me back to my car

350
00:22:56,360 --> 00:22:58,360
and I drove home

351
00:22:58,360 --> 00:23:00,360
and I never told the soul,

352
00:23:00,360 --> 00:23:02,360
because foresters don't get lost.

353
00:23:02,360 --> 00:23:04,360
But the point is

354
00:23:04,360 --> 00:23:06,360
the way I survived, that

355
00:23:06,360 --> 00:23:08,360
was simply because:

356
00:23:08,360 --> 00:23:10,360
one, I had a compass.

357
00:23:10,360 --> 00:23:13,360
Two, I knew if I headed west I would hit the ocean.

358
00:23:13,360 --> 00:23:16,360
Three, I had what they call a survival PMA

359
00:23:16,360 --> 00:23:18,360
or positive mental attitude.

360
00:23:18,360 --> 00:23:20,360
and another interesting point I want to make:

361
00:23:20,360 --> 00:23:22,360
When I started all this out,

362
00:23:22,360 --> 00:23:25,360
we didn't have satellite phones and GPS units

363
00:23:25,360 --> 00:23:28,360
And these wilderness trips were different. then

364
00:23:28,360 --> 00:23:30,360
You had to be really careful.

365
00:23:30,360 --> 00:23:33,360
If you got lost, you were lost.

366
00:23:33,360 --> 00:23:35,360
I mean, you better be able to figure out

367
00:23:35,360 --> 00:23:37,360
where you were with a map and a compass,

368
00:23:37,360 --> 00:23:40,360
because you couldn't call anybody.

369
00:23:40,360 --> 00:23:42,360
Then they came out with the satellite phone,

370
00:23:42,360 --> 00:23:46,360
which has totally changed the nature of wilderness travel.

371
00:23:46,360 --> 00:23:49,360
Now, in some respects it's made things safer

372
00:23:49,360 --> 00:23:52,360
and in other respects it's made it a way less safe.

373
00:23:52,360 --> 00:23:54,360
You can depend on your gear to a point,

374
00:23:54,360 --> 00:23:57,360
but if you don't have the skills

375
00:23:57,360 --> 00:23:59,360
and the judgment,

376
00:23:59,360 --> 00:24:02,360
the gear is just so much dead weight, isn't it?

377
00:24:02,360 --> 00:24:05,360
There's another factor we could add into this.

378
00:24:05,360 --> 00:24:08,360
I call this downplay your skills.

379
00:24:08,360 --> 00:24:10,360
I guess what I'm saying is

380
00:24:10,360 --> 00:24:13,360
my skills at paddling these rapids

381
00:24:13,360 --> 00:24:17,360
are way better than I'm willing to accept.

382
00:24:17,360 --> 00:24:23,360
I may be a class 3, 4 whitewater capable paddler,

383
00:24:23,360 --> 00:24:27,360
but if it gets into low class 3,

384
00:24:27,360 --> 00:24:30,360
I want to either portage or line

385
00:24:30,360 --> 00:24:32,360
or find some safe way around it.

386
00:24:32,360 --> 00:24:36,360
So judgment is really important.

387
00:24:36,360 --> 00:24:38,360
It takes a long time to develop judgment.

388
00:24:38,360 --> 00:24:40,360
You're not going to do that in one or two seasons.

389
00:24:40,360 --> 00:24:43,360
I wish the BSA would publish

390
00:24:43,360 --> 00:24:45,360
like an annual safety report

391
00:24:45,360 --> 00:24:48,360
with all the personal information expunged

392
00:24:48,360 --> 00:24:50,360
but just as an analysis

393
00:24:50,360 --> 00:24:52,360
what happened,

394
00:24:52,360 --> 00:24:54,360
maybe some conclusions about things

395
00:24:54,360 --> 00:24:56,360
that could have been done differently

396
00:24:56,360 --> 00:24:57,360
or a different approach,

397
00:24:57,360 --> 00:24:59,360
or where things kind of went south on somebody.

398
00:24:59,360 --> 00:25:01,360
That's a good idea.

399
00:25:01,360 --> 00:25:04,360
I'm very interested in becoming a good judge

400
00:25:04,360 --> 00:25:06,360
of what's smart and what's not,

401
00:25:06,360 --> 00:25:08,360
How to keep people safe and alive

402
00:25:08,360 --> 00:25:10,360
when you're out there,

403
00:25:10,360 --> 00:25:12,360
And that's one of the things

404
00:25:12,360 --> 00:25:14,360
I really value about your writing, too,

405
00:25:14,360 --> 00:25:17,360
is that's just in the background all the time.

406
00:25:17,360 --> 00:25:20,360
They do that for whitewater accidents,

407
00:25:20,360 --> 00:25:23,360
for what people are drowning

408
00:25:23,360 --> 00:25:25,360
and things like that in whitewater.

409
00:25:25,360 --> 00:25:27,360
I think that would be a really good idea.

410
00:25:27,360 --> 00:25:30,360
You've led some pretty amazing expeditions

411
00:25:30,360 --> 00:25:32,360
up into Northern Canada.

412
00:25:32,360 --> 00:25:34,360
Is there anyone that stands out

413
00:25:34,360 --> 00:25:36,360
as being the most memorable for you?

414
00:25:36,360 --> 00:25:37,360
Oh yeah,

415
00:25:37,360 --> 00:25:39,360
The Hood River, of course.

416
00:25:39,360 --> 00:25:41,360
That's where Susie and I got married.

417
00:25:41,360 --> 00:25:42,360
the Second Hood River.

418
00:25:42,360 --> 00:25:43,360
Tell me a little bit about that.

419
00:25:43,360 --> 00:25:46,360
The wedding story is actually in expedition canoeing,

420
00:25:46,360 --> 00:25:49,360
but the Hood is a magical river

421
00:25:49,360 --> 00:25:54,360
because it combines muskox, caribou,

422
00:25:54,360 --> 00:25:57,360
grizzly bears with thundering falls,

423
00:25:57,360 --> 00:25:59,360
huge falls- I mean Wilberforce Falls,

424
00:25:59,360 --> 00:26:00,360
where we were married-

425
00:26:00,360 --> 00:26:03,360
drops 160 feet through a three-mile canyon.

426
00:26:03,360 --> 00:26:06,360
I mean US Niagara drops 167 feet,

427
00:26:06,360 --> 00:26:08,360
so you can see the magnitude

428
00:26:08,360 --> 00:26:10,360
and that's like the third highest falls

429
00:26:10,360 --> 00:26:12,360
in North America, I believe.

430
00:26:12,360 --> 00:26:14,360
Well, that was a magical trip

431
00:26:14,360 --> 00:26:16,360
because Susie and I got married on it.

432
00:26:16,360 --> 00:26:19,360
The other trip that I was most memorable to me

433
00:26:19,360 --> 00:26:23,360
was the North Knife River in Manitoba,

434
00:26:23,360 --> 00:26:25,360
floating to Hudson Bay.

435
00:26:25,360 --> 00:26:27,360
I've actually done the North Knife

436
00:26:27,360 --> 00:26:29,360
three times or four times, I can't remember.

437
00:26:29,360 --> 00:26:32,360
But on the last trip we were chased.

438
00:26:32,360 --> 00:26:35,360
We were chased down the river by a polar bear.

439
00:26:35,360 --> 00:26:38,360
A polar bear came on land after us.

440
00:26:38,360 --> 00:26:41,360
We had a fire, a shot below his nose.

441
00:26:41,360 --> 00:26:43,360
That was a pretty scary thing.

442
00:26:44,360 --> 00:26:46,360
One of the trips on the North Knife

443
00:26:46,360 --> 00:26:49,360
we saw 11 polar bears.

444
00:26:49,360 --> 00:26:51,360
I would say those two,

445
00:26:51,360 --> 00:26:53,360
the Hood and the North Knife,

446
00:26:53,360 --> 00:26:56,360
are two of my most memorable rivers.

447
00:26:56,360 --> 00:26:59,360
But you know, every river is memorable.

448
00:26:59,360 --> 00:27:01,360
All of them are wonderful.

449
00:27:01,360 --> 00:27:03,360
I go to the Bounty Waters

450
00:27:03,360 --> 00:27:05,360
and I love that too.

451
00:27:05,360 --> 00:27:08,360
the Bounty Waters isn't as adventurous

452
00:27:08,360 --> 00:27:11,360
as Northern Canada but it's much more relaxing.

453
00:27:11,360 --> 00:27:15,360
I don't know. they all begin to flow together after a while.

454
00:27:15,360 --> 00:27:17,360
The Hood River

455
00:27:17,360 --> 00:27:20,360
just kind of put a pin in the map on that for me.

456
00:27:20,360 --> 00:27:22,360
You have to go first of all

457
00:27:22,360 --> 00:27:24,360
to the Northwest Territories of Canada

458
00:27:24,360 --> 00:27:26,360
and then you go to Yellow Knife,

459
00:27:26,360 --> 00:27:30,360
which would be the capital of the Northwest Territories,

460
00:27:30,360 --> 00:27:32,360
Almost to the North. roughly

461
00:27:32,360 --> 00:27:36,360
you'll go to Bathurst Inlet on the Arctic Ocean

462
00:27:36,360 --> 00:27:38,360
and that's where the Hood River ends.

463
00:27:38,360 --> 00:27:41,360
It ends at Bathurst Inlet on the Arctic Ocean.

464
00:27:41,360 --> 00:27:42,360
So you are out there.

465
00:27:42,360 --> 00:27:44,360
Yeah, you're above the Arctic Circle.

466
00:27:44,360 --> 00:27:47,360
We did a river in the Yukon called the Snake.

467
00:27:47,360 --> 00:27:49,360
the Snake River is above the Arctic Circle too.

468
00:27:49,360 --> 00:27:51,360
That's even further north in the Hood.

469
00:27:51,360 --> 00:27:54,360
There's something about the province of Nunavut,

470
00:27:54,360 --> 00:27:57,360
Northwest Territories and the new province of Nunavut

471
00:27:57,360 --> 00:28:00,360
that I just love. the topography,

472
00:28:00,360 --> 00:28:01,360
I don't know why.

473
00:28:01,360 --> 00:28:02,360
The wildlife is incredible.

474
00:28:02,360 --> 00:28:04,360
I mean tens of thousands,

475
00:28:04,360 --> 00:28:06,360
hundreds of thousands of caribou,

476
00:28:06,360 --> 00:28:10,360
and when caribou decide they want to cross a body of water,

477
00:28:10,360 --> 00:28:12,360
the alpha male looks around,

478
00:28:12,360 --> 00:28:14,360
looks around, and when he decides to go

479
00:28:14,360 --> 00:28:18,360
he jumps in and then they just all blindly follow.

480
00:28:18,360 --> 00:28:21,360
So we're coming around a bend

481
00:28:21,360 --> 00:28:23,360
and we're sort of single-filing along the left edge

482
00:28:23,360 --> 00:28:25,360
because there's a rapid ahead.

483
00:28:25,360 --> 00:28:28,360
The caribou are on this hilltop right to our left,

484
00:28:28,360 --> 00:28:29,360
thousands of them.

485
00:28:29,360 --> 00:28:31,360
The alpha male comes over

486
00:28:31,360 --> 00:28:33,360
and he looks down into the water.

487
00:28:33,360 --> 00:28:34,360
He doesn't see the canoes

488
00:28:34,360 --> 00:28:35,360
because we're too close to the bank.

489
00:28:35,360 --> 00:28:38,360
And then he jumps and when he jumps

490
00:28:38,360 --> 00:28:43,360
his hooves miss Susie's head by just a foot or so.

491
00:28:43,360 --> 00:28:45,360
I can feel the air, I mean the back.

492
00:28:45,360 --> 00:28:48,360
I can feel the wind from his body hitting me in the face.

493
00:28:48,360 --> 00:28:51,360
The other one of your books I keep close to hand

494
00:28:51,360 --> 00:28:53,360
and one of my favorite camping books

495
00:28:53,360 --> 00:28:55,360
is Camping's Top Secrets.

496
00:28:55,360 --> 00:28:56,360
I like that book too.

497
00:28:56,360 --> 00:28:57,360
That's a fun.

498
00:28:57,360 --> 00:28:59,360
It's in its third edition now

499
00:28:59,360 --> 00:29:02,360
and you keep revising and going over stuff

500
00:29:02,360 --> 00:29:04,360
so it stays very relevant.

501
00:29:04,360 --> 00:29:07,360
You have a favorite tip or piece of advice from the book?

502
00:29:07,360 --> 00:29:08,360
Yeah, I guess I do,

503
00:29:08,360 --> 00:29:11,360
And it's one of those bits of advice. that is,

504
00:29:11,360 --> 00:29:13,360
it's a constant battle with people

505
00:29:13,360 --> 00:29:16,360
because they refuse to acknowledge it,

506
00:29:16,360 --> 00:29:20,360
And it's this business of always put a ground cloth-

507
00:29:20,360 --> 00:29:23,360
plastic ground cloth- inside your tent,

508
00:29:23,360 --> 00:29:25,360
not under the floor.

509
00:29:25,360 --> 00:29:29,360
And so I do battle with people all the time over this.

510
00:29:29,360 --> 00:29:32,360
I like to tell those folks out there,

511
00:29:32,360 --> 00:29:36,360
like how floors got put into tents to begin with.

512
00:29:36,360 --> 00:29:39,360
Floors were not put into tents

513
00:29:39,360 --> 00:29:43,360
to keep stuff out of the tent, believe it or not.

514
00:29:43,360 --> 00:29:45,360
But setting a tent up without a floor,

515
00:29:45,360 --> 00:29:47,360
you've got to know what you're doing.

516
00:29:47,360 --> 00:29:49,360
Otherwise you keep moving stakes around

517
00:29:49,360 --> 00:29:52,360
because it's a rectangle and unsupported rectangle.

518
00:29:52,360 --> 00:29:54,360
So somebody got the idea to put a floor in a tent.

519
00:29:54,360 --> 00:29:57,360
so we just literally set the floor pattern

520
00:29:57,360 --> 00:29:59,360
so you could get the tent up easier.

521
00:29:59,360 --> 00:30:01,360
That's why floors were put in tents.

522
00:30:01,360 --> 00:30:04,360
The problem with the floor is

523
00:30:04,360 --> 00:30:06,360
any water that gets in the tent

524
00:30:06,360 --> 00:30:09,360
through seams in the floor stays inside the tent.

525
00:30:09,360 --> 00:30:11,360
So that's why you need a ground cloth

526
00:30:11,360 --> 00:30:14,360
inside the floor, not underneath it.

527
00:30:14,360 --> 00:30:17,360
But I would say that's the biggest,

528
00:30:17,360 --> 00:30:20,360
bestest tip that I can give anybody:

529
00:30:20,360 --> 00:30:24,360
Don't put a ground cloth underneath the floor of the tent.

530
00:30:24,360 --> 00:30:26,360
People think it protects the floor.

531
00:30:26,360 --> 00:30:28,360
Actually it doesn't.

532
00:30:28,360 --> 00:30:30,360
And I know all this sounds. this is all heresy

533
00:30:30,360 --> 00:30:32,360
I mean to a lot of people,

534
00:30:32,360 --> 00:30:35,360
And the way we discovered this was very interesting.

535
00:30:35,360 --> 00:30:37,360
You know what a green stick break is,

536
00:30:37,360 --> 00:30:39,360
Where you take a green stick between your hands

537
00:30:39,360 --> 00:30:41,360
and you bend it until it breaks. okay,

538
00:30:41,360 --> 00:30:43,360
The bottom compresses,

539
00:30:43,360 --> 00:30:45,360
The top elongates

540
00:30:45,360 --> 00:30:48,360
And then what happens is the top part snaps

541
00:30:48,360 --> 00:30:50,360
But the bottom doesn't.

542
00:30:50,360 --> 00:30:53,360
So a green stick always breaks from the top when it's bent.

543
00:30:53,360 --> 00:30:55,360
Well, that's the same thing that happens

544
00:30:55,360 --> 00:30:57,360
for the floor of a tent.

545
00:30:57,360 --> 00:30:59,360
The coating is on the inside of the floor.

546
00:30:59,360 --> 00:31:02,360
So when the stick starts poking up through the floor of the tent,

547
00:31:02,360 --> 00:31:05,360
what happens is the fabric stretches

548
00:31:05,360 --> 00:31:08,360
and the coating breaks at the top.

549
00:31:08,360 --> 00:31:11,360
If you put your ground cloth on the inside of the tent,

550
00:31:11,360 --> 00:31:13,360
it will prevent you from getting holes in the floor.

551
00:31:13,360 --> 00:31:15,360
And if you put holes in the floor

552
00:31:15,360 --> 00:31:17,360
and you have a ground cloth on the inside,

553
00:31:17,360 --> 00:31:20,360
it doesn't matter because you got a ground cloth on the inside.

554
00:31:20,360 --> 00:31:22,360
Exactly,

555
00:31:22,360 --> 00:31:24,360
If you put the ground cloth under the floor,

556
00:31:24,360 --> 00:31:26,360
then any water that gets trapped between the ground cloth

557
00:31:26,360 --> 00:31:28,360
and the floor gets pressurized.

558
00:31:28,360 --> 00:31:30,360
So I'd say that's my favorite tip.

559
00:31:30,360 --> 00:31:33,360
I'm definitely with you on the ground cloth inside of the tent.

560
00:31:33,360 --> 00:31:36,360
As a matter of fact, believe it or not,

561
00:31:36,360 --> 00:31:39,360
my fallback, if you didn't have a favorite tip,

562
00:31:39,360 --> 00:31:41,360
was to ask you about that one,

563
00:31:41,360 --> 00:31:43,360
because that's my favorite one.

564
00:31:43,360 --> 00:31:46,360
What I want you to do is you just tell your buddies once

565
00:31:46,360 --> 00:31:48,360
to do this and they won't do it.

566
00:31:48,360 --> 00:31:51,360
So what you do is, after you set up camp, that night,

567
00:31:51,360 --> 00:31:53,360
you go out on the little point out there,

568
00:31:53,360 --> 00:31:55,360
look towards the setting sun and you look up into the sky

569
00:31:55,360 --> 00:31:59,360
and say, oh God, please make it rain.

570
00:31:59,360 --> 00:32:03,360
And then, if it rains that night and they get flooded out

571
00:32:03,360 --> 00:32:06,360
and they'll see that you're not- they won't say a word,

572
00:32:06,360 --> 00:32:09,360
They will not say anything to you at all.

573
00:32:09,360 --> 00:32:11,360
From that point on, they'll always put the ground cloth

574
00:32:11,360 --> 00:32:13,360
inside the tent.

575
00:32:13,360 --> 00:32:16,360
I mean, I'm almost on a crusade about that.

576
00:32:16,360 --> 00:32:18,360
Well, you know, when I wrote that article

577
00:32:18,360 --> 00:32:20,360
about putting the ground cloth inside the tent,

578
00:32:20,360 --> 00:32:23,360
one of the editors from Scouting Magazine called me up

579
00:32:23,360 --> 00:32:25,360
and said: you know, don't you have this backwards clip?

580
00:32:25,360 --> 00:32:27,360
You know, is this an error?

581
00:32:27,360 --> 00:32:30,360
I said no, it's not an error,

582
00:32:30,360 --> 00:32:33,360
But, to their credit, they're willing to embrace new ideas

583
00:32:33,360 --> 00:32:35,360
and I think that's great.

584
00:32:35,360 --> 00:32:37,360
I saw this book maybe five or six years ago

585
00:32:37,360 --> 00:32:39,360
and it said Camping's Top Secrets,

586
00:32:39,360 --> 00:32:41,360
and I think the subtitle is

587
00:32:41,360 --> 00:32:45,360
a lexicon of camping tips. only the experts know,

588
00:32:45,360 --> 00:32:48,360
And I've been camping and going out for a number of years

589
00:32:48,360 --> 00:32:50,360
and I thought, yeah right,

590
00:32:50,360 --> 00:32:52,360
there's not going to be a whole lot in this,

591
00:32:52,360 --> 00:32:54,360
but I'll give the old guy a chance anyway.

592
00:32:54,360 --> 00:32:56,360
Oh, thanks,

593
00:32:56,360 --> 00:32:58,360
There was a lot I didn't know.

594
00:32:58,360 --> 00:32:59,360
Thank you.

595
00:32:59,360 --> 00:33:01,360
So it's a worthy read.

596
00:33:01,360 --> 00:33:03,360
It's a fun book too.

597
00:33:03,360 --> 00:33:04,360
Well, thank you.

598
00:33:04,360 --> 00:33:06,360
And then you can be converted to the sect

599
00:33:06,360 --> 00:33:09,360
of intent ground cloths

600
00:33:09,360 --> 00:33:12,360
and we'll trade stories back and forth,

601
00:33:12,360 --> 00:33:14,360
All right.

602
00:33:14,360 --> 00:33:17,360
What authors influence your writing

603
00:33:17,360 --> 00:33:19,360
and the way that you approach the outdoors?

604
00:33:19,360 --> 00:33:22,360
Well, the number one total mentor

605
00:33:22,360 --> 00:33:24,360
was Calvin Rottstrom.

606
00:33:24,360 --> 00:33:26,360
His books are, by the way,

607
00:33:26,360 --> 00:33:29,360
are back in print through University of Minnesota Press

608
00:33:29,360 --> 00:33:32,360
and I would encourage folks to read them:

609
00:33:32,360 --> 00:33:34,360
North American canoe country,

610
00:33:34,360 --> 00:33:36,360
New Way to Wilderness,

611
00:33:36,360 --> 00:33:38,360
Wilderness Route Finder

612
00:33:38,360 --> 00:33:41,360
and Paradise Below Zero.

613
00:33:41,360 --> 00:33:43,360
He wrote a whole slew of them

614
00:33:43,360 --> 00:33:45,360
and I just loved the guy,

615
00:33:45,360 --> 00:33:47,360
and actually his first book

616
00:33:47,360 --> 00:33:49,360
called The Way of the Wilderness.

617
00:33:49,360 --> 00:33:52,360
He was working for a Boy Scout camp,

618
00:33:52,360 --> 00:33:54,360
I think in Minnesota,

619
00:33:54,360 --> 00:33:56,360
and they asked him to write a pamphlet

620
00:33:56,360 --> 00:33:58,360
for the boys,

621
00:33:58,360 --> 00:34:00,360
and so he wrote this pamphlet thing

622
00:34:00,360 --> 00:34:01,360
called The Way of the Wilderness

623
00:34:01,360 --> 00:34:03,360
and it was so popular that it became

624
00:34:03,360 --> 00:34:04,360
he turned it into a book

625
00:34:04,360 --> 00:34:06,360
called The New Way of the Wilderness.

626
00:34:06,360 --> 00:34:08,360
Sig Olson,

627
00:34:08,360 --> 00:34:10,360
another very famous Minnesota writer.

628
00:34:10,360 --> 00:34:12,360
he was more of a descriptive writer.

629
00:34:12,360 --> 00:34:14,360
He would describe the shimmering fish

630
00:34:14,360 --> 00:34:16,360
in the moonlight.

631
00:34:16,360 --> 00:34:19,360
He would tell you what hook and line to use to catch them.

632
00:34:19,360 --> 00:34:21,360
I was really a Rottstrom fan

633
00:34:21,360 --> 00:34:23,360
and he was really number one for me,

634
00:34:23,360 --> 00:34:26,360
followed by a Horace Capehart

635
00:34:26,360 --> 00:34:29,360
who wrote Camping and Woodcraft in 1917.

636
00:34:29,360 --> 00:34:31,360
It was kind of interesting.

637
00:34:31,360 --> 00:34:35,360
In 1917, Capehart had a little kit,

638
00:34:35,360 --> 00:34:38,360
his daily kit, that he went.

639
00:34:38,360 --> 00:34:40,360
I think it was like something like nine pounds.

640
00:34:40,360 --> 00:34:42,360
That was his total kit.

641
00:34:42,360 --> 00:34:44,360
I'm going light today. give me a break.

642
00:34:44,360 --> 00:34:47,360
One of the things that Capehart observed in 1917

643
00:34:47,360 --> 00:34:50,360
was that insect headnets

644
00:34:50,360 --> 00:34:52,360
should be colored black,

645
00:34:52,360 --> 00:34:54,360
and the same thing on tents.

646
00:34:54,360 --> 00:34:56,360
Look out the screen of a tent

647
00:34:56,360 --> 00:34:58,360
with light colored netting

648
00:34:58,360 --> 00:35:01,360
and it reflects the lights right back into your eyes. again.

649
00:35:01,360 --> 00:35:03,360
With black colored netting

650
00:35:03,360 --> 00:35:05,360
you can see clearly.

651
00:35:05,360 --> 00:35:07,360
I tell people, just take a black magic marker

652
00:35:07,360 --> 00:35:09,360
and color the netting black.

653
00:35:09,360 --> 00:35:11,360
That's not a bad idea,

654
00:35:11,360 --> 00:35:13,360
That's a great idea,

655
00:35:13,360 --> 00:35:15,360
It works.

656
00:35:15,360 --> 00:35:17,360
Just color the eye panel black

657
00:35:17,360 --> 00:35:19,360
and all of a sudden you'll see clearly.

658
00:35:19,360 --> 00:35:21,360
Another one also was a guy,

659
00:35:21,360 --> 00:35:23,360
but his pen name was Nesmic.

660
00:35:23,360 --> 00:35:25,360
It was George Washington Sears.

661
00:35:25,360 --> 00:35:27,360
He lived out in the Adirondacks

662
00:35:27,360 --> 00:35:30,360
and he wrote a book called Woodcraft and Camping in 1920.

663
00:35:30,360 --> 00:35:32,360
It was interesting.

664
00:35:32,360 --> 00:35:34,360
His Capehart book was Camping and Woodcraft

665
00:35:34,360 --> 00:35:36,360
and Nesmic was Woodcraft and Camping.

666
00:35:36,360 --> 00:35:39,360
I guess they didn't care too much about tales.

667
00:35:39,360 --> 00:35:41,360
Those were really the three guys

668
00:35:41,360 --> 00:35:43,360
that inspired me most.

669
00:35:43,360 --> 00:35:45,360
What are you working on right now?

670
00:35:45,360 --> 00:35:47,360
Any new books in the works?

671
00:35:47,360 --> 00:35:49,360
I have Under Revision

672
00:35:49,360 --> 00:35:51,360
where we're revising the Bounty Waters book.

673
00:35:51,360 --> 00:35:53,360
It's going to be a huge, major revision

674
00:35:53,360 --> 00:35:55,360
Going the full color.

675
00:35:55,360 --> 00:35:57,360
It'll be a very nice book,

676
00:35:57,360 --> 00:35:59,360
But it won't be out till next year,

677
00:35:59,360 --> 00:36:02,360
I think in the spring of 2012..

678
00:36:02,360 --> 00:36:04,360
Basically, what I'm happy doing,

679
00:36:04,360 --> 00:36:07,360
I'm happy keeping my books in print.

680
00:36:07,360 --> 00:36:09,360
I really love writing for

681
00:36:09,360 --> 00:36:11,360
the Bladescouts,

682
00:36:11,360 --> 00:36:13,360
But I don't want to chain myself

683
00:36:13,360 --> 00:36:15,360
to a computer anymore.

684
00:36:15,360 --> 00:36:17,360
I'm getting to the age now

685
00:36:17,360 --> 00:36:19,360
where I want to play.

686
00:36:19,360 --> 00:36:21,360
I don't want to look at a computer screen,

687
00:36:21,360 --> 00:36:23,360
But I still enjoy talking to groups

688
00:36:23,360 --> 00:36:25,360
and giving presentations and things like that.

689
00:36:25,360 --> 00:36:27,360
That's a lot of fun for me.

690
00:36:27,360 --> 00:36:29,360
You're headed off for the Rio Grande

691
00:36:29,360 --> 00:36:31,360
Yep in about a week here.

692
00:36:31,360 --> 00:36:33,360
That'll be great.

693
00:36:33,360 --> 00:36:35,360
Once again, I really appreciate your time.

694
00:36:35,360 --> 00:36:37,360
Thanks for talking with us.

695
00:36:37,360 --> 00:36:39,360
Thanks, Clark.

